euthydemus-learn.md:5: argue/readings: S3 is read 2 ways: euthydemus-learn.logic.r1 has nothing from it; euthydemus-learn.logic.r1.2 and euthydemus-learn.logic.r1.3 have "learner"; they differ when nothing is true, which the first allows
euthydemus-learn.md:5: argue/answered: S3: answered no, which keeps euthydemus-learn.logic.r1.2 and euthydemus-learn.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "Cleinias was learning from the grammar-master and the lyre-master" is false. Is S3, "And they are the teachers of those who learn--the grammar-master and the lyre-master used to teach you and other boys; and you were the learners?", true then?
euthydemus-learn.md:13: argue/readings: S7 is read 2 ways: euthydemus-learn.logic.r1 and euthydemus-learn.logic.r1.2 have nothing from it; euthydemus-learn.logic.r1.3 has "¬wise_k"; they differ when only "Cleinias was wise, in the sense of knowing what he was learning" is true, which the first allows
euthydemus-learn.md:13: argue/answered: S7: answered no, which keeps euthydemus-learn.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "Cleinias was wise, in the sense of knowing what he was learning" is true. Is S7, "And were you wise then?", true then?
euthydemus-learn.md:15: argue/readings: S8 is read 3 ways: euthydemus-learn.logic.r1 has "¬knew → ¬wise_k"; euthydemus-learn.logic.r1.2 has "learner → ¬wise_k"; they differ when only "Cleinias was learning from the grammar-master and the lyre-master", "Cleinias knew the things which he was learning" and "Cleinias was wise, in the sense of knowing what he was learning" are true, which the first allows; euthydemus-learn.logic.r1.3 has nothing from it
euthydemus-learn.md:15: argue/answered: S8: answered no, which keeps euthydemus-learn.logic.r1.2, to: Suppose "Cleinias was learning from the grammar-master and the lyre-master", "Cleinias knew the things which he was learning" and "Cleinias was wise, in the sense of knowing what he was learning" are true. Is S8, "No, indeed, he said.", true then?
euthydemus-learn.md:27: argue/readings: S15 is read 2 ways: euthydemus-learn.logic.r1 has nothing from it; euthydemus-learn.logic.r1.2 and euthydemus-learn.logic.r1.3 have "learner_d"; they differ when nothing is true, which the first allows
euthydemus-learn.md:27: argue/answered: S15: answered yes, which keeps euthydemus-learn.logic.r1, to: Suppose "Cleinias learned what the grammar-master dictated" is false. Is S15, "Then, before the youth had time to recover his breath, Dionysodorus cleverly took him in hand, and said: Yes, Cleinias; and when the grammar-master dictated anything to you, were they the wise boys or the unlearned who learned the dictation?", true then?
euthydemus-learn.md:29: argue/readings: S16 is read 3 ways: euthydemus-learn.logic.r1 has "learner_d → wise_c"; euthydemus-learn.logic.r1.2 has "learner_d → wise_c ∧ ¬unlearned_c"; they differ when only "Cleinias learned what the grammar-master dictated", "Cleinias was among the wise boys, in the sense of clever or quick pupils" and "Cleinias was among the unlearned boys, in the sense of dull pupils" are true, which the first allows; euthydemus-learn.logic.r1.3 has "wise_c ∧ learner_d"
euthydemus-learn.md:29: argue/answered: S16: answered yes, which keeps euthydemus-learn.logic.r1 and euthydemus-learn.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "Cleinias learned what the grammar-master dictated", "Cleinias was among the wise boys, in the sense of clever or quick pupils" and "Cleinias was among the unlearned boys, in the sense of dull pupils" are true. Is S16, "The wise, replied Cleinias.", true then?
euthydemus-learn.logic.r1: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: euthydemus-learn.logic.r1 and euthydemus-learn.logic.r1.3 have nothing from it; euthydemus-learn.logic.r1.2 has "wise_k → knew"; they differ when only "Cleinias was wise, in the sense of knowing what he was learning" is true, which the first allows
euthydemus-learn.md:25: argue/readings: the conclusion is read 3 ways: euthydemus-learn.logic.r1 has "learner → unlearned_k ∧ ¬wise_k"; euthydemus-learn.logic.r1.2 has "wise_c ∧ ¬unlearned_c ∧ ¬unlearned_k"; they differ when only "Cleinias was learning from the grammar-master and the lyre-master" and "Cleinias was unlearned, in the sense of not knowing what he was learning" are true, which the first allows; euthydemus-learn.logic.r1.3 has "learner_d ∧ wise_c ∧ ¬(learner ∧ unlearned_k)"
euthydemus-learn.logic.r1: argue/readings: the main conclusion is read at 2 sentences: euthydemus-learn.logic.r1 puts it at S13, "Then the unlearned learn, and not the wise, Cleinias, as you imagine."; euthydemus-learn.logic.r1.2 and euthydemus-learn.logic.r1.3 at S17, "Then after all the wise are the learners and not the unlearned; and your last answer to Euthydemus was wrong."
euthydemus-learn.logic.r1: argue/answered: M: answered no, which keeps euthydemus-learn.logic.r1.2 and euthydemus-learn.logic.r1.3, to: Is the text's main conclusion S13, "Then the unlearned learn, and not the wise, Cleinias, as you imagine.", rather than S17, "Then after all the wise are the learners and not the unlearned; and your last answer to Euthydemus was wrong."?
